\section{Introduction}

General Relativity established that gravity is geometry. The presence of mass-energy curves spacetime, and what we experience as gravitational attraction is motion along geodesics in that curved space. This was not a description of gravity acting through geometry — it was the replacement of gravity-as-force with geometry-as-physics.

That move was radical and it worked. But it left a question open that has not been closed in the century since: \textit{is the geometry fundamental, or does it emerge from something deeper?}

A parallel program, developed independently across thermodynamics, quantum information, and quantum gravity, has been assembling evidence for the latter position. Jacobson showed in 1995 that the Einstein field equations follow from thermodynamics of local spacetime patches — gravity as an equation of state, not a fundamental law \cite{jacobson1995}. Verlinde extended this in 2010 and 2016, deriving Newtonian gravity and its relativistic extension from entropic considerations on holographic screens \cite{verlinde2010, verlinde2016}. Maldacena and Susskind proposed in 2013 that quantum entanglement and spacetime geometry are equivalent descriptions of the same structure \cite{maldacena2013}.

The thread connecting these results is information. Not information as metaphor, but information in the technical sense: entropy, distinguishability, causal structure. The proposal emerging from this thread is that geometry is what information flow looks like when you project it onto the scales we measure.

This paper takes that proposal seriously and asks what follows from it — specifically for the fundamental forces and for the observed acceleration of cosmic expansion.

We are explicit about what this is. GUES is a conjecture at Evidence Level E1: structured speculation with falsification paths proposed but not yet tested. Nothing here has passed a Gate 3 or above under the governing methodology \cite{charter}. The value of this document is not proof — it is the honest structuring of an intuition that connects to established physics at multiple points and deserves rigorous examination.

