\section{Foundational Framing}

\subsection{Information Flow as Given}

A boundary condition of this conjecture: we treat information flow as given. We do not ask what generates distinguishability, what produces initial conditions, or what is upstream of the informational substrate. These are legitimate questions. They are not questions this paper attempts to answer.

This is the same move thermodynamics makes. Entropy is given. The second law describes what follows from it. The source of the asymmetry is a separate inquiry.

By taking information flow as given, we make the conjecture tractable and falsifiable at the level we are working. Claims that require specifying the source of information are deferred to other work.

\subsection{The Geometry-Information Relationship}

Let $\mathcal{I}$ denote an information flow structure — a manifold of distinguishable states with causal ordering and entropy gradients. We propose:

\begin{conjecture}[Emergent Geometry]
Spacetime geometry $g_{\mu\nu}$ is not fundamental but emerges from the structure of $\mathcal{I}$. Specifically, the metric at any point is determined by the local entropy gradient structure of the information flow, such that:
\begin{equation}
G_{\mu\nu} = \kappa \, T^{(\mathcal{I})}_{\mu\nu}
\end{equation}
where $T^{(\mathcal{I})}_{\mu\nu}$ is the stress-energy tensor of the information flow field, and $G_{\mu\nu}$ is the Einstein tensor of the emergent metric.
\end{conjecture}

This is not new mathematics. It is a reframing of Jacobson's result \cite{jacobson1995}: the Einstein equations already follow from thermodynamic considerations. What we add is the explicit identification of information flow — rather than local Rindler horizons — as the generating structure.

\textbf{Falsification path (E1):} If geometry is emergent from information flow rather than fundamental, deviations from smooth Lorentzian manifold behavior should appear at regions of maximum entropy gradient — near black hole horizons, in early-universe conditions, at the Planck scale. Current measurements from LIGO gravitational wave observations and Event Horizon Telescope imaging constrain the smoothness of spacetime near horizons. A quantitative prediction of the deviation magnitude and scale is required to advance this claim to E2.

