\section{Forces as Symmetry Structures in Emergent Geometry}

\subsection{The Standard Model Gauge Groups}

The fundamental forces of the Standard Model correspond to gauge symmetry groups:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Electromagnetism: $U(1)$
  \item Weak force: $SU(2)$
  \item Strong force: $SU(3)$
\end{itemize}

These are not arbitrary choices. They are the symmetry structures that, when gauged, produce the observed interactions. The question of why these specific groups — and not others — is not answered by the Standard Model itself. It is a boundary condition, not a result.

\subsection{Forces as Geometric Cross-Sections}

\begin{conjecture}[Force Symmetries as Geometry]
The gauge groups $U(1)$, $SU(2)$, and $SU(3)$ are not independently fundamental. They are cross-sections — faces — of a single higher-dimensional geometric object $\mathcal{G}$ within the emergent geometry. Different dimensional projections of $\mathcal{G}$ yield different gauge symmetry structures.
\end{conjecture}

The intuition is geometric and can be stated plainly: a triangle, square, and pentagon are not three unrelated objects. They can be faces of a single higher-dimensional solid. The gauge groups of the Standard Model may stand in an analogous relationship.

This is not without precedent. The original Kaluza-Klein program showed that adding one spatial dimension to GR yields electromagnetism automatically \cite{kaluza1921, klein1926}. String theory and M-theory extend this program. The specific higher-dimensional object $\mathcal{G}$ is not identified in this paper.

\speculative{The identification of $\mathcal{G}$ is the central open problem for this conjecture. Candidate structures include $E_8$, $G_2$, and novel objects arising from the information-flow substrate. This requires mathematical work beyond the scope of v0.1.}

\textbf{Falsification path (E1):} If the gauge groups are faces of a single geometric object, there should exist symmetry-breaking pathways connecting them — not just at high energy (as in Grand Unified Theories) but through geometric transitions in the emergent structure. The electroweak unification already demonstrates that $U(1)$ and $SU(2)$ merge at sufficient energy. The extension to $SU(3)$ and the identification of the containing object $\mathcal{G}$ is the testable claim.

