\section{Black Holes as Information-to-Geometry Transducers}

\subsection{The Standard Picture and Its Gap}

In standard cosmology, black holes are endpoints — regions where information falls in and, via Hawking radiation, slowly leaks out as thermal noise \cite{hawking1974, bekenstein1973}. The information paradox — whether information is truly lost — remains unresolved.

The holographic principle, encoded in the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy formula:
\begin{equation}
S_{BH} = \frac{k_B c^3}{4 G \hbar} A
\end{equation}
establishes that the information content of a black hole is proportional to its horizon area, not its volume. Information is encoded on the boundary, not the interior.

\subsection{Geometric Output}

\begin{conjecture}[Black Holes as Geometric Transducers]
Black holes do not merely absorb information and re-emit it thermally. They function as transducers — converting high-entropy infalling information into structured low-entropy geometric output that propagates into the surrounding manifold. This geometric output is distinct from Hawking radiation and non-radiative in the electromagnetic sense.
\end{conjecture}

The proposed mechanism, at E1:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Infalling matter encodes its information structure on the event horizon (holographic principle)
  \item The extreme spacetime curvature at the horizon acts as a compression operator on that information structure
  \item The compressed, structured information is emitted as geometric perturbations — updates to the local causal structure of spacetime
  \item Accumulated over cosmological time and across the black hole population, these geometric emissions contribute a pressure term to the expansion of spacetime
\end{enumerate}

\speculative{Step 3 — the emission of geometric perturbations distinct from Hawking radiation — is the least specified part of this mechanism and the point most requiring mathematical development.}

\subsection{Dark Energy as Accumulated Geometric Pressure}

The observed acceleration of cosmic expansion is currently attributed to dark energy, parameterized as a cosmological constant $\Lambda$ in the Friedmann equations:
\begin{equation}
H^2 = \frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho + \frac{\Lambda}{3}
\end{equation}

The physical mechanism of $\Lambda$ is unknown. Vacuum energy calculations predict a value approximately $10^{120}$ times larger than observed — the cosmological constant problem.

\begin{conjecture}[Geometric Expansion Pressure]
The cosmological constant $\Lambda$ is not vacuum energy but accumulated geometric output from the black hole population of the observable universe. The expansion rate should correlate with black hole density distribution at cosmological scales.
\end{conjecture}

\textbf{Falsification path (E1):} If expansion is driven by geometric output from black holes, the local expansion rate should correlate with local black hole density. Regions of higher supermassive black hole density should show measurably higher local expansion rates, independent of mass distribution effects. This prediction is distinguishable in principle from $\Lambda$CDM, which predicts uniform $\Lambda$. Current data from large-scale structure surveys and black hole catalogs could in principle test this correlation.

\speculative{The quantitative prediction — what correlation coefficient, at what scale, with what measurement precision — has not been specified. This is required for G1 closure under the Charter.}

